@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/Jenkins/Views/Shared/JenkinsLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Native OS Installation";
}

<style>
    #native-os-installation-page pre {
        background-color: #212529 !important;
        color: #ffffff !important;
        border-radius: 8px;
        padding: 16px !important;
        overflow-x: auto;
        margin-top: 12px;
    }

        #native-os-installation-page pre code {
            background: transparent !important;
            color: #ffffff !important;
        }

    #native-os-installation-page .concept-box {
        background: #f8f9fa;
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        border-radius: 12px;
        padding: 24px;
    }

    #native-os-installation-page .info-card {
        background: #ffffff;
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        border-radius: 10px;
        padding: 18px;
        height: 100%;
    }

        #native-os-installation-page .info-card h5 {
            color: #5b21b6;
            font-weight: 700;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }

    #native-os-installation-page .example-box {
        background: #f8f9fa;
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        border-radius: 10px;
        padding: 20px;
    }
</style>

<main id="native-os-installation-page" class="container py-5">

    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/Jenkins/Views/Shared/JenkinsTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- MAIN CONTENT -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <!-- PAGE TITLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h1 class="fw-bold">Native OS Installation</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    Learn how to install Jenkins directly on host operating systems using native package managers and manage it as a persistent system background service.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">What is a Native OS Installation?</h2>
                <p>
                    A Native OS Installation involves running Jenkins directly inside your host's operating system environment rather than wrapping it in an isolated container. Jenkins configures itself as a system daemon or background process (such as a <code>systemd</code> service on Linux or a standard Windows Service), allowing it to boot up automatically when the server starts.
                </p>

                <div class="alert alert-info">
                    <strong>Production Note:</strong> Native installations are highly favored by enterprise infrastructure teams who prefer standard system monitoring tools, localized OS configurations, and direct access to server host hardware resources.
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- KEY CONCEPTS -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Key Concepts By Platform</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">

                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>1. Debian / Ubuntu Linux</h5>
                            <p class="mb-2">Uses official apt repositories. Requires importing the GPG authentication key before adding the Jenkins source list.</p>
                            <strong>Package Manager:</strong> <code>apt-get install jenkins</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>2. Red Hat / CentOS / Fedora</h5>
                            <p class="mb-2">Configured by pulling down the official Jenkins yum configuration file and importing the corresponding Red Hat key.</p>
                            <strong>Package Manager:</strong> <code>yum install jenkins</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>3. Microsoft Windows</h5>
                            <p class="mb-2">Distributed as a standard graphical installer (<code>.msi</code>). It guides users through setup wizard screens and configures a background Windows Service.</p>
                            <strong>Service Context:</strong> <code>services.msc</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>4. Service Orchestration</h5>
                            <p class="mb-2">On modern Linux nodes, Jenkins relies heavily on system managers to gracefully start, stop, restart, or evaluate daemon status hooks.</p>
                            <strong>Daemon Utility:</strong> <code>systemctl</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- EXAMPLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Ubuntu Linux Installation Script Example</h2>
                <p>The following workflow shows how to register the official Jenkins LTS (Long-Term Support) repository key, inject the repository into your system package manager list, and trigger the install sequence:</p>
                <pre><code># 1. Download and register the official Jenkins repository GPG signing key
sudo wget -O /usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc \
  https://jenkins.io













# 2. Append the authenticated Debian package repository to the host source configurations
echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] \
  https://jenkins.io binary/" | sudo tee \
  /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null

# 3. Synchronize package index indices and deploy the application service binary
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install jenkins -y

# 4. Initialize and evaluate the state of the native system background daemon
sudo systemctl start jenkins
sudo systemctl status jenkins</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- PRACTICE -->
                <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practice Exercise</h2>
            <ol>
                    <li class="mb-2">Log into your target target test Linux server infrastructure utilizing an SSH client connection.</li>
            <li class="mb-2">Follow the sequential system package commands detailed in the example script above to register and download the binary.</li>
            <li class="mb-2">Run <code>sudo systemctl status jenkins</code> to guarantee your automation engine state flag evaluates as <strong>active (running)</strong>.</li>
                <li>Locate your default administrator security entry password string by reading the initial initialization setup path: <br /><code>sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword</code>.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

        <!-- SUMMARY -->
            <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
    <h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">You have completed the <strong>Native OS Installation</strong> lesson! You now know how to deploy Jenkins directly to an operating system as a managed service, handle keys, and inspect background service statuses.</p>
</div>


<!-- NAVIGATION -->
<div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
<a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="@Url.Action("DockerInstallation", "Foundations", new { area = "Jenkins" })">← Previous: Jenkins with Docker</a>
<a class="btn btn-primary" href="@Url.Action("Dashboard", "Foundations", new { area = "Jenkins" })">Next: Jenkins Dashboard →</a>
</div>

</section>

</div>

</main>
